```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
Location C (PC11–PC14, 192.168.200.0/24) is not isolated. Direct testing
disproves the helpdesk claim: PC11 successfully pings its gateway
(192.168.200.1), reaches PC4 in the 172.16.10.0/24 segment, and traces a
clean 3-hop path to router0 (192.168.1.1) via router4 → router5 → router0.
The "totally cut off" description is a false premise.

The only connectivity gap is between Location C and Location D
(172.18.50–51.0/24, served by router6). A traceroute from PC11 to PC15
(172.18.51.15) reaches router6 at hop 3 (10.50.1.2) and then goes silent.
Inspection of router6's iptables FORWARD chain reveals the cause: a
deliberately configured ACL that drops all TCP, UDP, and ICMP packets
forwarded inbound from eth2 (the 10.50.1.0/24 uplink to router3), with a
single narrow exception permitting HTTP from 172.16.10.0/24 to 172.18.51.1
on port 80. The DROP counters on router6 show active hits (6 ICMP packets
matching my test pings), confirming the ACL is engaged and is the sole
blocker. This ACL is an intentional security policy in the Kath3 baseline
configuration, not an injected anomaly.

Corrective action: none required. Location C's routing and infrastructure are
functioning correctly. If users genuinely need access to Location D, that
would require a deliberate policy change on router6 (adding permit rules for
the required sources/services before the DROP statements), not a fault
remediation.
=== END ===
```